    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the city-capacity rule for `POST /api/owners` following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions, mirroring the existing `EnsureUnique*` steps rather than growing any class.\n\n**New files:**\n- `EnsureCityCapacity.java` \u2014 a single-responsibility step that reads the validated request (`@Val OwnerFieldsDto`), and throws `CityAtCapacityException` when `ownerRepository.countByCity(city) >= 50`. The `50` threshold is a named `CAPACITY` constant.\n- `CityAtCapacityException.java` \u2014 a checked exception (extends `Exception`) so it surfaces in the `throws` clause.\n- `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `409 Conflict` with `{\"errors\":[\"city\"]}`, matching the shape of the existing duplicate-telephone/household handlers.\n- Escalation YAML `...CityAtCapacityException.yml` wiring the exception to its handler.\n\n**Wiring:**\n- Inserted an `ensureCityCapacity` step into `owners.POST.yml` right after `ensureUniqueHousehold` and before `normalizeEmail` \u2014 i.e. among the pre-`build` validation checks, before the transaction/save steps, so a full city is rejected without any write.\n\nReused the pre-existing `OwnerRepository.countByCity(...)` (already used by `AssignCustomerCode`) rather than adding a new query. The acceptance test `coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fill a city to 50, expect the 51st to 409) and the full suite pass.",
